        "2": {
          "id": "2",
          "name": "vision.md",
          "contents": "I want a page about the Westview Wolves, my favorite local basketball team. Make it dark and bold using the team colors: navy blue and gold.\n\nThe page should have the team name at the top, a section about the team's history, and a list of the starting players.",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        }
      },
      "openFiles": [
        "1",
        "2"
      ]
    },
    "offer_browser_tts": "true",
    "teacher_markdown": "**Intent:** A second hand-edit, one notch subtler than Maya's. The bug is a contrast failure caused by inheritance: `body` sets `background-color: #003366` *and* `color: #003366`, so every element that inherits its text color disappears - except the `h1`, which has its own gold rule. Students must connect what the Tag Inspector taught (styles flow down from containers) to a real repair. The fix is one value. This is also a quiet accessibility lesson: contrast is what makes a page readable.\r\n\r\n**Circulation questions:**\r\n\r\n- \"Why is the title readable when nothing else is?\"\r\n- \"The paragraph has no color rule of its own - where is its color coming from?\"\r\n- \"You changed one line and the whole page fixed itself. Why?\"\r\n\r\n**Expected mistakes and misconceptions:**\r\n\r\n- Students may add a color rule to every element instead of fixing `body` - both work; ask them which is less code and why\r\n- Students may lose the team colors while fixing readability - remind them the vision says navy and gold; light gold text on navy keeps both\r\n- Students may not connect this to inheritance - ask them to hover the equivalent element on the last level's inspector in their memory: \"who did the paragraph get its color from?\"\r\n",
    "require_edit_to_continue": "true"
  },
